<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
	xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
	<modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

	<!--
	    The add-on update site, published to GitHub Pages.

	    Distinct from the repository bundle/ builds: that one also carries the
	    Rodin platform and the product, so installing from it into an existing
	    Rodin would offer a second copy of Rodin itself.  This site offers only
	    add-on features, which is what someone running a stock Rodin wants.

	    It deliberately omits org.eventb.smt.  Our SMT plug-in calls
	    BasicTactics.firstSuccessful and looks up the tactic combinator
	    org.eventb.core.seqprover.firstSuccessful, both ISP RAS additions that
	    live only in our rodincore fork.  Stock Rodin ships
	    org.eventb.core.seqprover under the same 3.8.0 version without them, so
	    p2 would install the plug-in happily and it would then fail the first
	    time the tactic ran.  SMT ships in the bundle, where the platform is
	    ours.

	    Building the remaining two here, in one module, is also what keeps the
	    category tree single.  Each plug-in repository has its own site module
	    with its own category id, both labelled "Event-B Tools"; Tycho
	    qualifies category IU ids per build, so composing those sites shows the
	    label twice.  One module, built once, cannot.
	-->
	<parent>
		<groupId>org.eventb</groupId>
		<artifactId>parent</artifactId>
		<version>3.10.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
		<relativePath>../rodincore/org.eventb.parent/pom.xml</relativePath>
	</parent>

	<groupId>org.eventb.rossi</groupId>
	<artifactId>updates</artifactId>
	<version>3.10.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
	<packaging>eclipse-repository</packaging>

	<name>Rodin Add-ons Update Site (eventb-rossi)</name>

</project>
